<h3 class="sub">Timeline: how an Indian war game became chess</h3>
<ul class="tl">
<li><b>About 6th century CE, Gupta-era India:</b> Chaturanga appears, with pieces modelled on the four divisions of the army.</li>
<li><b>Persia:</b> it becomes <i>Chatrang</i>. The call <i>shah mat</i> ("the king is helpless") gives us the word checkmate.</li>
<li><b>Arab world:</b> it becomes <i>Shatranj</i> and spreads across North Africa and into Spain.</li>
<li><b>Medieval Europe:</b> chess is adopted, and by the late 15th century the queen and bishop gain their modern powers.</li>
<li><b>Moksha Patam to Snakes and Ladders:</b> the Indian moral game reached Britain in the Victorian era and was sold worldwide.</li>
<li><b>Pachisi to Ludo:</b> tradition says Mughal emperor Akbar played a life-size Pachisi in the courtyards of Fatehpur Sikri. A simplified version became Ludo.</li>
</ul>
</div></section>

<section id="strategy" class="alt"><div class="wrap">
<h2><span class="eb">Level 2</span>Strategy</h2>
<p class="lead">Texts such as Kautilya's <i>Arthashastra</i> and the <i>Dhanurveda</i> describe army organisation, spies, terrain and formations called <i>Vyuhas</i>. Select a formation to see how it works.</p>
<div class="split">
<div>
<div class="tabs" role="group" aria-label="Choose formation">
<button aria-pressed="true" data-v="chakra">Chakravyuha</button>
<button aria-pressed="false" data-v="pad">Padmavyuha</button>
<button aria-pressed="false" data-v="garuda">Garudavyuha</button>
</div>
<div class="vy"><svg id="vsvg" viewBox="0 0 300 300" role="img" aria-label="Formation diagram"></svg></div>
</div>
<div style="color:#bdbdbd"><h3 id="vt" style="color:#fff"></h3><p id="vd"></p><p id="vm"></p></div>
</div>
<h3 class="sub" style="color:#fff">Kautilya's toolkit, mapped to game mechanics</h3>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Saptanga: 7 elements of a state</h3><p>King, minister, territory, fort, treasury, army and ally. In games: leader unit, tech tree, map, base, gold, units and teammates.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Four upayas</h3><p><i>Sama</i> (talk), <i>dana</i> (gift), <i>bheda</i> (divide), <i>danda</i> (force). Strategy games offer the same options: diplomacy, trade, sabotage, attack.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Mandala theory</h3><p>Your neighbour is a likely enemy, and the neighbour's neighbour is a likely friend. This is how alliances form in games like Civilization (our own interpretation).</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Sadgunya: 6 policies</h3><p>Peace, war, neutrality, march, alliance and double policy. A player picks one depending on relative strength.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Spies and intelligence</h3><p>The Arthashastra describes networks of agents. Scouting, fog of war and recon in games work the same way.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Dhanurveda: weapons</h3><p>The tradition classifies weapons as thrown, hand-held, both, and mechanical. Game loadouts use similar categories.</p></div>
</div></div></section>

<section id="modern"><div class="wrap">
<h2><span class="eb">Level 3</span>Modern warfare</h2>
<p class="lead">Games such as Call of Duty, Age of Empires, Clash of Clans and PUBG or BGMI use the same ideas the old texts describe.</p>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card m"><h3>Squads and roles</h3><p>Assault, support and sniper roles work like the four divisions of the Chaturangini Sena.</p></div>
<div class="card m"><h3>Map control</h3><p>Holding high ground and chokepoints follows the Arthashastra advice on choosing terrain.</p></div>
<div class="card m"><h3>Resources</h3><p>Ammo, gold and supply lines match the Arthashastra's focus on treasury and logistics.</p></div>
<div class="card m"><h3>Recon and intel</h3><p>Drones and scouting echo the spy networks (<i>gudhapurusha</i>) of Kautilya.</p></div>
</div>
<h3 class="sub">Four games, four ancient ideas</h3>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<tr><th>Game</th><th>Core idea</th><th>Ancient Indian link</th></tr>
<tr><td>Call of Duty</td><td>Squad-based shooter with roles and objectives</td><td>Specialised army divisions and capturing key points</td></tr>
<tr><td>Age of Empires</td><td>Gather resources, build an army, advance through ages</td><td>Kosha (treasury) and danda (army) from Saptanga</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clash of Clans</td><td>Layered base defence against raids</td><td>Padmavyuha and fort (<i>durga</i>) defence</td></tr>
<tr><td>PUBG / BGMI</td><td>Shrinking safe zone forces players together</td><td>Chakravyuha encirclement</td></tr>
</table></div>
</div></section>

