<div class="card m"><h3>The story</h3><p>Raji is an orphaned circus performer. When demons attack and a new war between gods and demons begins, her brother Golu is taken. The goddess Durga supports her, while Vishnu has doubts. Her path leads to the demon lord Mahabalasura.</p></div>
<div class="card m"><h3>Mythology</h3><p>The game draws on the Mahabharata and Ramayana, and mixes Hindu and Balinese mythology. It is one of the first big games set in ancient India.</p></div>
<div class="card m"><h3>Weapons from the gods</h3><p>Raji receives weapons and powers from the gods, starting with a <i>trishul</i> (trident) and later the <i>chakra</i>. This echoes the divine <i>astras</i> of the epics and the Dhanurveda.</p></div>
<div class="card m"><h3>Art style</h3><p>The world uses the Pahari painting style, with hand-painted textures on 3D models. Environments are inspired by medieval forts and palaces of Rajasthan.</p></div>
<div class="card m"><h3>Storytelling in stone</h3><p>The developers use wall carvings and paintings to tell myths through animated environments. Games like Journey and Bastion also influenced its look and feel.</p></div>
<div class="card m"><h3>Why it matters</h3><p>The team has said they felt a void of games based on Indian culture and wanted to show it in depth. Raji proves that IKS can inspire a global game.</p></div>
</div>
